# The Convex deployment the frontend talks to. COMMITTED ON PURPOSE.
#
# These two are NEXT_PUBLIC_*, which means Next.js inlines them into the
# JavaScript bundle it serves to every browser. They are public by
# construction — the value is already readable by anyone who loads tom.quest,
# so keeping it in the gitignored secrets/ directory protected nothing and cost
# something real: no environment without Vercel credentials could build at all.
# A Claude session on the Jarvis Box has a checkout and no Vercel login, so
# `pnpm build` failed for every one of them at the prerender step.
#
# WHY THE BUILD IS RIGHT TO FAIL WITHOUT THEM, and why this file rather than a
# lazier check: an inlined value that is missing at BUILD time is missing from
# the shipped bundle. Deferring the error to runtime would turn a loud build
# failure into a silent broken deploy, which is the trade C3 (fail-loud) exists
# to refuse. So the fix is to make the value available, never to soften the
# check in app/lib/auth.tsx.
#
# PRECEDENCE, so this file can never override a real deployment: Next.js does
# not overwrite a variable already present in the process environment, so
# Vercel's project env wins on Vercel and .env.local wins locally. This is the
# fallback that makes a bare checkout buildable.
#
# Secrets do NOT belong here. secrets/next.env remains the one home for every
# Next-side value that is actually secret (CONVEX_DEPLOY_KEY, SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN,
# TURING_API_KEY, …), pushed by `pnpm secrets:sync`. That script fails loudly if
# Vercel's copy of either value below ever drifts from this file.

NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL=https://admired-chinchilla-140.convex.cloud
NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_SITE_URL=https://admired-chinchilla-140.convex.site
